T["train.html"] = """{% extends "_base.html" %}{% block body %}
Train ML and how the score works
SICCA does not use a black-box model. The score is a transparent Bayesian calculation you can inspect, change and challenge. A machine-learning step exists only as an optional calibration, and only when you have real labelled data. Nothing here is trained by default, and no accuracy is claimed.
Model status: {% if model %}Calibration trained on {{ model.n }} pairs on {{ model.trained }} (slope {{ '%.2f'|format(model.a) }}, intercept {{ '%.2f'|format(model.b) }}, in-sample Brier {{ '%.3f'|format(model.brier_in_sample) }}). {% else %}No calibration model. Scores are uncalibrated structured estimates.{% endif %}
Contents
1 What the score means | 2 Bayes in plain words | 3 The prior | 4 Evidence and likelihood ratios | 5 Status and reliability | 6 Dependent evidence | 7 Putting it together | 8 Worked example | 9 Uncertainty interval | 10 Sensitivity | 11 Baseline rank | 12 Calibration and training | 13 Fact checks | 14 Limits | 15 Glossary
1. What the score means
The headline number answers one question: given what has been entered, how likely is it that the two cases share a perpetrator or operating group? It is a degree of belief that is updated by evidence. It is not a measured frequency, not a verdict and not evidence in itself. A weighted average of subjective factor scores (as used in earlier SICCA reports) is not a probability, because it has no baseline and no stated meaning for "0.42". This version replaces it.
Only the linkage question is scored. "No links whatsoever" is a much wider claim: weak overlaps such as the same region or even the same bar can exist without any common offender. The score measures the strong claim, a shared perpetrator or cell.
2. Bayes in plain words
Start with how likely a link was before looking at anything specific (the prior). Then, for each piece of evidence, ask: how much more likely would I see this if the cases were linked than if they were not? That ratio is the likelihood ratio (LR). A ratio of 1 means the evidence says nothing. Above 1 supports a link. Below 1 argues against.
posterior odds = prior odds x LR1 x LR2 x LR3 ...
odds = p / (1 - p) and p = odds / (1 + odds)
Working in odds lets independent pieces of evidence multiply. Two items with LR 2 and 1.5 together give 3.
3. The prior
The prior is your starting probability of a shared perpetrator before specific evidence. It should reflect how often two unrelated crimes of this kind in the same area turn out to be committed by the same person or group. For two crimes with very different methods, four years apart, a low prior such as 1 to 3% is reasonable. The default is {{ '%.0f'|format(default_prior*100) }}%.
The prior matters a lot. A dramatic prior (say 50%) will make almost any evidence look convincing, so always check how the result changes when you move it. If you cannot justify your prior in a sentence, the estimate is only as good as that guess.
4. Evidence items and default likelihood ratios
Each item belongs to a category with a default LR, which you can override. These defaults are judgment calls, not measured statistics. They are ordered by how hard the evidence is to explain by chance.
Category	Default LR	Meaning{% for k,v in cats.items() %}
{{ k }}	{{ v[0] }}	{{ v[1] }}{% endfor %}
Why coincidences score 1.0. A shared calendar date, a street name or the number of victims has no causal mechanism: nobody controls when a body is found. Such items are recorded but do not move the estimate unless you give them a reason to. Two events that match by chance are exactly what you would expect to find among many comparable cases.
Why contradictions matter. Evidence against a link (LR below 1) is recorded just as supportive evidence is. Different cause of death, different method, or a person shown to be elsewhere all belong here.
5. Status and reliability discount
Not every claim is equally trustworthy. Before an item is used, its LR is shrunk toward 1 by how much you trust it:
effective LR = LR ^ (status weight x reliability)
Status	Weight	Use it when
verified	{{ sw.verified }}	Primary record: court judgment, forensic report, official file
witness	{{ sw.witness }}	A named witness said so, not independently confirmed
unverified	{{ sw.unverified }}	Press repetition, books, rumour with no primary source
disputed	{{ sw.disputed }}	Sources contradict each other on this point
Reliability (0 to 1) is your confidence in the specific source. An exponent of 0 gives an effective LR of 1, meaning the item is ignored. An exponent of 1 uses the full LR. This keeps a repeated press claim from counting as much as a court record.
6. Dependent evidence (no double counting)
Several items often describe the same underlying fact. "Same region", "same hills" and "same terrain" are really one observation. Give such items the same group name. Within a group, only the item with the largest effect is counted. The table on the Network page shows "no (group)" for items that were set aside. Leaving the group blank treats the item as independent, so use groups when in doubt.
7. Putting it together
    1. Convert each item to an effective LR (section 5).
    2. Keep the strongest item per dependency group (section 6).
    3. Multiply the remaining LRs to get the combined LR.
    4. Multiply by the prior odds and convert back to a probability.
The system also adds a few automatic items from the case records (same crime type, location text overlap, long time gap). These are weak by design and labelled auto.
8. Worked example (calculated live)
Prior {{ '%.0f'|format(ex.prior*100) }}%. Two items: a bar that a witness placed at both scenes (LR 2, witness, reliability 0.8), and a shared terrain (LR 1.5, verified, reliability 1.0).
Item	LR	Exponent	Effective LR {% for i in ex['items'] %}
{{ i.description }}	{{ i.lr }}	{{ '%.2f'|format(i.w) }}	{{ '%.3f'|format(i.elr) }}{% endfor %}
Combined LR {{ '%.3f'|format(ex.combined_lr) }}. Estimate {{ '%.1f'|format(ex.post*100) }}%, 90% interval {{ '%.1f'|format(ex.p5*100) }}% to {{ '%.1f'|format(ex.p95*100) }}%. Two modest, partly trusted items lift a 2% prior only to about {{ '%.0f'|format(ex.post*100) }}%: a link stays unlikely unless strong, verified evidence appears.
9. Uncertainty interval
A single number hides how unsure the inputs are. The interval comes from a Monte Carlo simulation of {{ n_mc }} runs: each run jitters the prior (log-normal, spread 0.5) and every counted LR (in log space). Weakly trusted items are jittered more, strongly verified items less. The 5th to 95th percentile of the results is the 90% interval. A wide interval means the conclusion depends on soft inputs and should not be quoted as a precise figure.
10. Sensitivity
The sensitivity table removes each counted item in turn and reports how many percentage points the estimate changes. It shows which single claim carries the result. If one unverified item drives everything, verifying that item is the most valuable next step.
11. Baseline rank
A score only means something relative to what unrelated cases would score. The rank compares this pair's combined evidence with every other case pair in the database. With fewer than 10 pairs the figure is hidden, because a small library cannot show what "random" looks like. Load more unrelated cases from the same region to make the baseline useful. Note that records you entered yourself share your biases: a pair you studied closely will always look richer than pairs you did not.
12. Calibration and training
Calibration checks whether "30%" actually means about 3 in 10. It requires labelled pairs: cases where the truth about linkage is known (for example solved cases with confirmed common or separate offenders). SICCA then fits a one-dimensional logistic curve (Platt scaling) p = 1 / (1 + exp(-(a x log-odds + b))) by gradient descent and reports an in-sample Brier score (mean squared error of the probabilities; lower is better, 0.25 is a coin flip). The fitted values are saved as signed JSON, never as a pickle file, so a tampered file is rejected.
Requirements: a CSV with columns log_odds (the natural log of the posterior odds from this tool) and label (1 if the pair was truly linked, 0 if not), at least 30 rows and both labels present. With the two or three cases you may have now, do not train: any fitted curve would be noise. The in-sample Brier score is optimistic; validate on held-out pairs before relying on it.
{% if current_user.is_admin() %}
""" + CSRF + """
{% else %}
Only administrators can train.
{% endif %}
13. Automatic fact checks
When you open a case or a comparison, SICCA compares your description text with the structured data and warns about mismatches, such as a description saying the victims were girls while a male victim is recorded, or a stated victim count that differs from the victims entered. These checks catch typing and copy errors; they do not verify facts against the outside world.
14. Limits and misuse
    • Every LR and prior is a human judgment. The output is as good as those judgments and the sources behind them. 
    • Items are only partly independent even with groups; the product can overstate certainty. 
    • Press accounts often copy each other. Ten articles repeating one claim are still one claim: give them one group and an unverified status. 
    • Anchoring risk: once a link feels plausible, it is easy to add supportive items and skip contradicting ones. Record contradictions too. 
    • A low estimate does not prove cases are unrelated, and a high one does not prove they are related. Neither is evidence in a legal sense. 
    • Do not name or accuse individuals on the basis of this score.
15. Glossary
Prior	Probability before specific evidence.
Odds	p / (1 - p). 2% is odds of about 0.02 to 1.
Likelihood ratio	How much likelier the evidence is if the cases are linked than if not.
Posterior	Probability after combining prior and evidence.
Calibration	Whether stated probabilities match real frequencies.
Brier score	Mean squared error between probabilities and outcomes.
Monte Carlo	Repeated random re-runs to see how results vary with uncertain inputs.
Base rate	How often something happens in comparable situations overall.
{% endblock %}""" 
